WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.016
Seven years ago. John had an affair
with a woman at work.

2
00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:10.416
JOHN: I've been
offered voluntary redundancy.

3
00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:13.256
I need to do more at work or Martin
is never gonna make me a partner.

4
00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:14.496
It could be good for us.

5
00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:17.456
You need to do the handover to the
officer who'll wrap up this case.

6
00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:19.936
GUNSHOT Fine. Who is he?

7
00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:21.456
She.

8
00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:25.176
Before he died. I heard him arguing
with someone about "the General".

9
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:27.216
That's Fatemah Feyzi.

10
00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:28.736
BEESTON: 'There'll be casualties.

11
00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:32.440
'That's what you want. Isn't it?
To make a splash.'

12
00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:49.000
CLAIRE: So. the first question
is "appreciation".

13
00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:52.536
Well. do you wanna go first?
OK.

14
00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:56.776
So. I appreciate that you're
considering the redundancy.

15
00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:58.000
Your turn.

16
00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:02.776
I appreciated that blow job
you gave me back in 2014.

17
00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:05.240
You're meant to be taking this
seriously. All right.

18
00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:10.920
I appreciate you always
giving me a second chance.

19
00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:15.096
I mean it. really.

20
00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:17.680
This was a good idea. What's next?

21
00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:21.296
A puzzle.

22
00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:22.576
Something that puzzles you

23
00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:24.840
or that you don't understand
about your partner.

24
00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:32.320
Do you wish that you'd married
someone a bit more like Martin?

25
00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:36.960
The idea of someone else. it's...

26
00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:39.680
it's complicated.

27
00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:42.000
What?

28
00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:49.520
It's OK to say that sometimes
I do wish we had a more normal life.

29
00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:53.120
I thought you'd say "No".

30
00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:56.000
Would you rather I just lied?

31
00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:00.200
Mummy?

32
00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:03.000
I'll go.

33
00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:08.640
Come on. mate.
let's get you dressed.

34
00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:12.880
HORN HONKS

35
00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:17.480
RUDY: So. I've decrypted everything
on Ehsan's laptop.

36
00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:23.296
We have numerous documents
in English and Farsi.

37
00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:26.856
Lots of imagery.
video imagery. pictures.

38
00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:29.456
social media screen grabs.
we blinks. you name it.

39
00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:31.856
In every single one.
as far as I can see.

40
00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:34.096
Fatemah Feyzi is the subject.

41
00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:37.176
It does looks like Ehsan Taremi
was surveilling her.

42
00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:40.256
We know the Iranian army
used criminal gangs.

43
00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:43.496
like that run by Craig Beeston.
to carry out attacks on dissidents

44
00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:45.816
and media organisations
that speak out against them.

45
00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:47.160
Thanks for the brief.

46
00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:51.160
But I don't hear any apology
for going behind my back again.

47
00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:54.296
I was in Manchester with John.

48
00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:57.136
I understood he was onto something
and so I backed him. It's on me.

49
00:02:57.160 --> 00:02:58.496
I see.

50
00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:02.936
You're new. so it may be worth you
knowing that John and I go way back.

51
00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:06.056
and I can smell the blag
from a hundred paces.

52
00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:09.616
Ehsan Taremi warned me
that there are many lives at risk.

53
00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:11.736
Fatemah Feyzi
is giving a talk next week

54
00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:13.736
at a university in Liverpool
to 200 people.

55
00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:16.056
Now. we know there
are hardliners in Iran

56
00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:17.656
who are opposing
the UK's involvement

57
00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:19.296
in the denuclearisation talks.

58
00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:21.656
They wanna make a stand
by attacking more than one person.

59
00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:23.320
KNOCKING.
DOOR OPENS

60
00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:27.720
SIMONE CLEARS THROAT
Thank you. Emily.

61
00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:32.496
This would all need sign off
from senior management.

62
00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:34.536
Why? Why?
Come on.

63
00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:36.296
We'll look ridiculous
sounding the alarm

64
00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:39.200
about an Iranian terror plot
without any actual evidence.

65
00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:44.536
You look thirsty. John.
Shall I be mother?

66
00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:46.160
Why not?
You're only missing the pinny.

67
00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:49.416
You still having sugar. John?

68
00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:51.496
Two please. Gregory.
with a drop of ordinary milk.

69
00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:53.816
Ordinary milk?
Now. it's not a biggie.

70
00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:57.776
but we do know cow no longer assumes
the mantle of normative privilege

71
00:03:57.800 --> 00:03:59.656
over oat milk or soya?

72
00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:01.776
Do you know.
I find it deeply problematic.

73
00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:04.536
the way that you've deliberately
excluded almond milk from that.

74
00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:05.656
Then tell me. Gregory.

75
00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:07.736
who'll speak for the humble
and oppressed coconut?

76
00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:09.320
SIMONE CLEARS THROAT Sorry.

77
00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:11.920
What about your informant?

78
00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:16.456
Mona Taremi gave us a lead
on somebody called "the General".

79
00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:18.760
We were gonna speak
to Jordan about it. Thank you.

80
00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:28.696
OK. you can keep going with this
for the time being.

81
00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:30.576
but Mehreen is running the show.

82
00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:33.896
Go and talk to Fatemah.
And you keep in touch with me. yes?

83
00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:35.120
Will do.
Thank you.

84
00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:41.680
DOOR OPENS

85
00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:45.856
'What do you think about Greg?'

86
00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:47.680
Ordinary Milk Guy?
Hmm.

87
00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:51.216
He's obviously not
your best friend.

88
00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:54.656
I put evidence on the table
and he's still nit-picking.

89
00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:58.936
I mean. does he hate me
or is this something else?

90
00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:01.120
I think hate's a strong word. John.

91
00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:05.056
What if someone on the inside
of the organisation

92
00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:06.656
gave Beeston my address?

93
00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:10.056
Gangsters don't get people
inside the security service.

94
00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:13.496
My God. you are trouble.
Yeah. but what if?

95
00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:16.296
It's no wonder they're trying to
manage you out of the organisation.

96
00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:17.416
CHUCKLES

97
00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:20.256
Woman. Life. Freedom
is. to this day.

98
00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:23.816
the rallying cry for the right
to self-determination.

99
00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:25.216
And this is a struggle

100
00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:29.216
we must all be prepared to continue
for as long as necessary.

101
00:05:29.240 --> 00:05:33.376
We must be prepared to lead.
And I will.

102
00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:34.976
MANAGER: Back off!
BELL DINGS.

103
00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:36.336
CHATTER

104
00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:38.176
I had my first
Threat To Life Warning

105
00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:40.336
from the police ten years ago.

106
00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:43.296
I'm sorry. but if I stop
every time my life is in danger.

107
00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:44.536
I'd never say anything.

108
00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:47.656
This isn't only about
a potential threat to YOUR life.

109
00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:49.736
We believe
that they're targeting your talk

110
00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:51.696
at the Liverpool Central University.

111
00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:53.096
What's your evidence?

112
00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:55.296
We can't share
that information right now.

113
00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:58.056
If I don't see evidence.
I am not cancelling.

114
00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:01.856
I will not be intimidated
and neither will my supporters.

115
00:06:01.880 --> 00:06:03.736
We've been fighting for decades!

116
00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:06.920
And finally.
the West is hearing our voice.

117
00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:09.800
Do you know Ehsan Taremi?

118
00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:19.376
You gave a talk
at the Persian Women's Action Group.

119
00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:21.656
Did you see his wife.
Mona Taremi. there?

120
00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:26.016
I gave the talk a few months ago.
I don't recall meeting her.

121
00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:27.576
Now. if you'll excuse me...

122
00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:29.776
Are you sure you
don't remember Ehsan? He knew you.

123
00:06:29.800 --> 00:06:32.216
In fact. he took a great deal
of interest in your movements.

124
00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:34.256
Ehsan Taremi was murdered recently

125
00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:36.136
and we're trying
to establish a connection.

126
00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:37.200
Take another look.

127
00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:42.560
I'm sorry. I don't know him.

128
00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:47.056
Five kilos of Beeston's product
is missing.

129
00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:50.016
Mm-hm. I know. But I know that
this isn't a fallout over drugs.

130
00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:52.136
But there's an obvious motive
for killing Ehsan.

131
00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:53.856
Well. then what was that shit
with Beeston

132
00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:55.416
where he took us down
to the warehouse?

133
00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:57.776
They want their gear back
and they think Mona's got it.

134
00:06:57.800 --> 00:07:00.816
I think we need to speak
to Mona again. I agree.

135
00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:03.520
I think she's holding
something back from us. Mm.

136
00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:07.360
Must be miserable stuck in
a safe house for Persian New Year.

137
00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:13.000
How's she doing?
OFFICER: Yeah. OK. Go through.

138
00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:17.216
Hey. Mona. got you a coffee.

139
00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:20.016
Are we safe here?
Yeah. yeah. I promise you. you are.

140
00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:21.976
Look. I wanted to ask.

141
00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:26.736
did Ehsan ever mention any concerns
about Fatemah Feyzi's security?

142
00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:30.736
No. but everyone knows if you speak
against the government in Iran.

143
00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:32.216
you may face danger.

144
00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:34.456
We had some information that Ehsan

145
00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:37.896
may have stolen five kilos
of Craig Beeston's heroin.

146
00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:40.096
Ehsan... Ehsan wouldn't do that.

147
00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:43.096
After Ehsan was killed there was
a thorough search of your property.

148
00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:46.376
but I was wondering
if you had anywhere else.

149
00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:48.416
perhaps in someone else's name.

150
00:07:48.440 --> 00:07:52.040
A lock-up or a storage unit.
a bedsit?

151
00:07:55.280 --> 00:07:56.496
What is it?

152
00:07:56.520 --> 00:07:58.200
METAL DOOR RATTLES

153
00:08:47.720 --> 00:08:51.480
Ehsan was a good man.
He wouldn't take that filth.

154
00:09:09.040 --> 00:09:11.840
Forty-nine years.
So. what's the secret?

155
00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:16.496
You want an answer?

156
00:09:16.520 --> 00:09:18.936
I'm not sure we do. actually. Dad.

157
00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:21.296
Compatibility. shared interests.

158
00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:24.240
and understanding who's in charge.

159
00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:28.336
One also has to keep
the flame of passion...

160
00:09:28.360 --> 00:09:30.536
Oh. my God. No. Dad.
Graham. the dining table!

161
00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:32.856
No. no. you carry on. Graham.
Someone top his glass up.

162
00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:34.976
JOHN CHUCKLES

163
00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:36.256
How's your mother. John?

164
00:09:36.280 --> 00:09:38.496
Oh. she's good.
Thank you for asking.

165
00:09:38.520 --> 00:09:39.760
How are her legs?

166
00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:41.840
They're not so good.

167
00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:44.736
But she's got one of those
mobility scooters

168
00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:46.216
that she whizzes about on.

169
00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:48.696
Honestly. I swear she gets
up to 60 miles an hour.

170
00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:53.136
We really need to...
get up to see her with the kids.

171
00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:54.576
It's been a while.

172
00:09:54.600 --> 00:09:57.640
Claire was saying you're thinking
of a change of career.

173
00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:00.080
No.

174
00:10:03.920 --> 00:10:05.896
Well. I know you can't say anything.

175
00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:10.056
And. er... I don't know
whether there's anything

176
00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:12.696
to this offer
of voluntary redundancy.

177
00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:16.336
But you've given over 20 years
of service to the country.

178
00:10:16.360 --> 00:10:21.256
Graham. don't interfere. darling.
John's done his bit.

179
00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:24.776
Why not go into the private sector
and earn some money.

180
00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:27.880
and take some of the strain
off Claire?

181
00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:35.760
TOILET FLUSHING

182
00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:46.736
We always do things
with your family.

183
00:10:46.760 --> 00:10:48.680
Well. we do live in London.

184
00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:56.400
We should invite my mum down
for Christmas this year.

185
00:10:57.680 --> 00:10:59.616
Really?

186
00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:00.776
Why not?

187
00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:03.056
I don't mind her
coming down for Christmas.

188
00:11:03.080 --> 00:11:05.416
But I end up
running around after her.

189
00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:08.736
The last time. you went off for work
and left me with her and the kids.

190
00:11:08.760 --> 00:11:10.736
Kit was only three months old.

191
00:11:10.760 --> 00:11:12.280
All right. Then we won't invite her.

192
00:11:17.400 --> 00:11:20.416
Your dad really put me
on the spot tonight.

193
00:11:20.440 --> 00:11:23.976
I'm sorry about that.
But he's worried about me.

194
00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:26.560
You didn't tell me
you're not taking the redundancy.

195
00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:38.600
Martin offered me partner today.

196
00:11:42.640 --> 00:11:43.720
Did he?

197
00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:47.056
Well. that's great.
What did you say?

198
00:11:47.080 --> 00:11:49.256
I said thank you very much.

199
00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:52.496
but that I'd like to sleep on it
and discuss it with my husband.

200
00:11:52.520 --> 00:11:54.976
It's a lot more responsibility.
and Kit's still young. Yeah.

201
00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:57.256
But. no. maybe I should be
a bit more like you.

202
00:11:57.280 --> 00:12:00.216
Just out for myself.
Maybe my dad's right.

203
00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:02.176
Well. your dad.
he's always right. isn't he?

204
00:12:02.200 --> 00:12:05.280
Good relationships
are based on compatibility.

205
00:12:06.360 --> 00:12:08.256
And maybe we're just... we're not.

206
00:12:08.280 --> 00:12:10.656
And I'm sure your dad
would've been much happier

207
00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:12.280
if you'd married
someone like Martin.

208
00:12:21.080 --> 00:12:25.096
Unfortunately. Ehsan Taremi died
because he stole heroin

209
00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:28.056
with a street value
of maybe half-a-million pounds.

210
00:12:28.080 --> 00:12:31.456
Which. in Craig Beeston's world.
certainly guarantees you an OBE.

211
00:12:31.480 --> 00:12:33.880
GREG: An OBE?
One behind the ear.

212
00:12:35.320 --> 00:12:36.736
Well. I hate to say it.

213
00:12:36.760 --> 00:12:40.080
but I did mention you may have been
over-hyping this one. Congrats.

214
00:12:41.520 --> 00:12:42.696
SIMONE: Thank you. Mehreen.

215
00:12:42.720 --> 00:12:44.776
Your work's been first class
on this.

216
00:12:44.800 --> 00:12:47.336
I shall sing with an ever greater
joy at choir this evening.

217
00:12:47.360 --> 00:12:48.736
Knock yourself out. Greg.

218
00:12:48.760 --> 00:12:51.096
You should come along.
I bet you've a lovely singing voice.

219
00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:53.776
Called Stella's Angels.
Established in the Rimington years.

220
00:12:53.800 --> 00:12:56.560
Would you and Greg mind giving
John and I a moment alone. please?

221
00:13:06.120 --> 00:13:10.016
This just doesn't feel right.
I think that heroin was planted.

222
00:13:10.040 --> 00:13:11.856
Do you have any evidence
to back up this claim.

223
00:13:11.880 --> 00:13:13.376
or is this another gut feeling?

224
00:13:13.400 --> 00:13:16.480
What if Beeston was trying
to throw us off the trail?

225
00:13:18.520 --> 00:13:19.976
He plants the heroin to distract

226
00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:22.960
from the very real and present
threat to national security.

227
00:13:27.440 --> 00:13:30.200
Your judgement's been lacking
all the way lately.

228
00:13:31.640 --> 00:13:33.416
I'm your friend.
but I must warn you -

229
00:13:33.440 --> 00:13:35.416
things are not looking good for you.

230
00:13:35.440 --> 00:13:37.656
It's out of my hands.
so there will have to be an inquiry

231
00:13:37.680 --> 00:13:40.456
into Ehsan and his killer's death.
What for?

232
00:13:40.480 --> 00:13:41.816
I can hold off senior management

233
00:13:41.840 --> 00:13:43.656
as long as you're on your way
out the building.

234
00:13:43.680 --> 00:13:45.496
but if you're staying...
Then what?

235
00:13:45.520 --> 00:13:48.216
"You. Mr John Hughes. will be taken
to the village square

236
00:13:48.240 --> 00:13:49.880
"and publicly flogged." Is that it?

237
00:13:52.480 --> 00:13:56.576
You can't stay at State Threats.
I'll let you know your options.

238
00:13:56.600 --> 00:13:58.040
Such as they are.

239
00:14:08.520 --> 00:14:09.656
CLEARS THROAT

240
00:14:09.680 --> 00:14:10.880
You OK?

241
00:14:12.680 --> 00:14:14.696
Yeah. fine. You?

242
00:14:14.720 --> 00:14:17.696
Huh. I was meant
to be discussing the handover

